(Tutor: Hi Marcus, let's keep going with factoring. You had x^2 + 5x + 6 = 0 left over,) Tj T*
(    right?) Tj T*
(Marcus: Yeah. I wasn't sure where to start.) Tj T*
(Tutor: What do you think the first move is?) Tj T*
(Marcus: Um, divide by x?) Tj T*
(Tutor: Tell me what made you reach for that one.) Tj T*
(Marcus: Because there's an x in both of the first parts.) Tj T*
(Tutor: That makes sense as an instinct. The thing is, you'd lose a solution that way. What) Tj T*
(    we want is two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 5. What pairs multiply to 6?) Tj T*
(Marcus: 1 and 6. 2 and 3.) Tj T*
(Tutor: Good. Which of those adds to 5?) Tj T*
(Marcus: 2 and 3.) Tj T*
(Tutor: So what does that give you?) Tj T*
(Marcus: \(x + 2\)\(x + 3\) = 0?) Tj T*
(Tutor: Yes. Now what does it mean that the whole thing equals zero?) Tj T*
(Marcus: I don't know, you set them both to zero?) Tj T*
(Tutor: Right, you do. So x = -2 and x = -3. Nice.) Tj T*
(Marcus: Okay. What about x^2 - 49 = 0?) Tj T*
(Tutor: Same idea. Two numbers that multiply to -49 and add to 0.) Tj T*
(Marcus: 7 and -7?) Tj T*
(Tutor: Exactly. So x = 7 or x = -7.) Tj T*
(Marcus: Got it.) Tj T*
(Tutor: Let's do one more. x^2 + 8x + 15 = 0.) Tj T*
(Marcus: Multiply to 15, add to 8. 3 and 5.) Tj T*
(Tutor: Good. So?) Tj T*
(Marcus: x = -3 and x = -5.) Tj T*
(Tutor: That's right. You picked up that factoring pattern much faster than last week.) Tj T*
(Marcus: Thanks. Can my mom call you about scheduling? Her number is 555-412-9087.) Tj T*
(Tutor: Sure, or she can email me at tutor.help@mathcenter.org. See you Thursday.) Tj T*
ET
